<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dunkeld</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dunkeld is a small town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tay">River Tay</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perthshire">Perthshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. In the 9th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenneth-macalpin">Kenneth MacAlpin</a> is said to have brought relics of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> there, and it became a leading church centre of the kingdom of Alba. Its lay abbot Crínán was the father of King Duncan I, so the royal line that ruled until 1286 is known as the House of Dunkeld. Its partly ruined medieval cathedral still stands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dunkeld.jpg" alt="Dunkeld" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diliff · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dunkeld_Cathedral_Exterior,_Dunkeld,_UK_-_Diliff.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dunkeld lies on the north bank of the Tay opposite Birnam, close to the Highland Boundary Fault, about 25 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a> on the A9, and is often called the Gateway to the Highlands. Its name, from the Gaelic Dùn Chailleann, means fort of the Caledonians, probably the hill fort on King's Seat just north of the town, which suggests the place mattered as far back as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. Much of the surrounding countryside is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tay">River Tay</a> (Dunkeld) National Scenic Area, and the Birnam Oak nearby is believed to be the last tree of the Birnam Wood named in Shakespeare's Macbeth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Dunkeld is said to have been founded by Caustantín son of Fergus, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>, who died in 820, and a Pictish monastery stood there. The relics of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> were divided between Dunkeld and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kells">Kells</a> in Ireland to keep them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raiders. For a time Dunkeld was the chief church of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, a place it yielded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a> in the 10th century. Danish Vikings raided it in 903, but it flourished into the 11th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> revived the see, and by 1300 the diocese held sixty parish churches.</p><p>The cathedral choir dates to the 13th and 14th centuries, the nave to the early 15th, and the tower and chapter house to 1450 to 1475. It was stripped after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and its nave has been roofless since the early 17th century. On 21 August 1689 the Cameronian Regiment under William Cleland, who died in the fight, held the town against the Jacobites in the Battle of Dunkeld, which destroyed most of the old town. Thomas Telford's bridge of 1809 changed the town's layout.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The rebuilt town is one of the most complete 18th-century country towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, and the National Trust for Scotland has restored about twenty of its houses. The choir of the cathedral is still the parish church, the nave and tower are in the care of Historic Environment Scotland, and murals of about 1490 survive beneath the tower vault.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dunkeld” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 849 relics of Columba were moved to <b>Dunkeld</b>, and by about 900 the merged kingdom was called Alba.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>By about 900, under Donald II and Constantine II, the kingdom was called Alba, with its royal centre at Scone and relics of Columba at <b>Dunkeld</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Major religious sites of eastern Pictland included Portmahomack, Cennrígmonaid (later St Andrews), <b>Dunkeld</b>, Abernethy and Rosemarkie, which appear to be associated with Pictish kings.</blockquote><a href="/culture/picts">Picts · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>A small regiment of Cameronians held the town of <b>Dunkeld</b> against a much larger Jacobite force.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-21">21 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Dunkeld</b>, on its edge, became a major church centre in the 9th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-atholl">Kingdom of Atholl</a></li></ul></section>
